	That's a completely reasonable concern.  

	I could mumble vague reassurances about our sense of fair 
play and our splendidly good intentions.  Instead, I'll confidently
describe our lack of determination and our wretchedly poor record on
actually getting things done.  Let's be realistic, and look at what 
the future holds for these YANIs:

"The YANIs drop to the ground."  --more--
"*THUD*  The YANIs die."  --more--
"Do you want their bad points identified?  [y/n]"
 

In more detail:

	The Dev Team seems to be quite conservative.  I can see 
them throwing a big *addition* into the game without proper con-
sideration.  I doubt that they would make a big *change* to the
existing game balance without a lot of bick^H^H^H^H thought.

	The Dev Team sometimes accept YANIs from outside, IF the
YANI is coded up.  YANIs are rarely coded up.  When YANIs are coded 
up, it's nearly always by the originator.  

	In this thread, you only have to worry about goofball YANIs 
put forward by people who have actually written patches.  In this
thread, the least stable YANI-ite who has actually written a patch 
is... me.

	OK, let's go over a few classes of YANIs from this thread,
with particular attention to ones which might be implemented by
yours truly:


1)	New add-on levels, harder than the existing levels.

	I didn't actually talk about these in this thread, but I've
certainly had them in mind for a while.  These add-on levels would 
be along the lines of:  replacement Sokoban levels without the 
puzzles, but with very challenging monsters.  Challenging enough 
that expert players would usually have to retreat or die.

	I would have to return to my old hobby of designing levels,
first, but that's certainly possible.  Note, though, that my way of
distributing such levels lets you pick and choose the levels you 
add.  If the Dev Team changed Nethack's packaging to make it easy 
to use add-on levels, I would expect this feature to be carried 
forward.

	Hard add-on levels will quite likely be designed, and they
might even make it into the official release, but they would not 
affect you unless you turned 'em on.  (Ditto easy add-on levels,
like a Sokoban level with a general store instead of a puzzle.)


2)	Moving challenges from the early game to the late game.

> 	...still scratching around for a first ascension....  I
> lose good characters in gehennon...

	You are very, very close to ascending.  When learning how 
to ascend, getting to the Castle means that you're 90% of the way
there.  Unfortunately, when you count levels, you find that the
Castle is only 30% of the way to the end.  The last 70% of our 
journey of 1000 miles has only 10% of the scenery.  Dull.

	I feel that moving challenges to the late game is quite a 
good idea.  The journey of 1000 miles would still be 1000 miles, but
getting to the Castle would be significantly easier, and the last
700 miles would have 20% (!) of the scenery.  Still dull, but better.

	Some of the challenge-moving YANIs might actually get into
Nethack.  From where I'm sitting, they look like low-risk changes 
which directly address a known problem.  For you, a higher percent-
age of your characters would survive to the Castle, but the game 
would be a bit harder after that.  It would balance out.


3)	Random YANIs.

	Nothing else in this thread is likely to be coded up, even
by the originator.


4)	Bad YANIs.

	Especially not these.  Even by the originator.  (Some of 
those people are just firing off the first little thought to go
through their minds.  I swear, I've put more thought into this one
post than most of them have put into their YANIs, *combined*.)


	In summary, then:  most of these YANIs are bad, and we
know it.  We're too lazy to code up even the good YANIs.  The Dev 
Team ignores YANIs which haven't been coded up.  The Dev Team 
mostly ignores ideas from outsiders, anyway.  The Dev Team is very
conservative.  

	And, as the final, totally unrefutable answer to your
concern:

	In six months, you'll have ascended several times, just like
us; you'll be bored with Gehennom, just like us; and you'll be 
yarping and hooting for changes to the late game.

	Just like us.

